2026-06-30 15:00:48,663 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-cf489837-8934-42bd-8b7f-a8e446ec8231', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nLumos_RSLC2_Aldolase_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Aldolase_DSSO_Opt_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Aldolase_DSSO_Opt_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_BSA_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_BSA_DSSO_Opt_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_BSA_DSSO_Opt_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_BSA_DSSO_Opt_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Cas9_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Cas9_DSSO_Opt_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Cas9_DSSO_Opt_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Cas9_DSSO_Opt_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Conalbumin_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Myoglobin_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_TF_DSSO_Opt_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_TF_DSSO_Opt_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_TF_DSSO_Opt_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC2_Transferin_DSSO_CID-ETD_ID.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_Aldolase_DSSO_Opt_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS2_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS2_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS2_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS3_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS3_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_MS2MS3_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_stepped-HCD_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_stepped-HCD_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_BSA_stepped-HCD_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_Myoglobin_Optimization_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_Myoglobin_Optimization_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_Myoglobin_Optimization_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_stepped-HCD_comparison_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_stepped-HCD_comparison_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC3_stepped-HCD_comparison_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_CID_to_HCD_BSA_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_CID_to_HCD_BSA_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_CID_to_HCD_BSA_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_DSBU_Opt_BSA_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_DSBU_Opt_BSA_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_DSBU_Opt_BSA_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F5_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F5_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC6_Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F5_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F3_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F3_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F3_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F5_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Tryp-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F5_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_Typ-GluC_steppedHCD_SEC_F5_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8__Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8__Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8__Tryp-GluC_CID-HCD_SEC_F4_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F3_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F3_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F3_3.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_1.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_2.raw\nLumos_RSLC8_steppedHCD_SEC_F4_3.raw\nQExHFX4_RSLC3_BSA_DSBU.raw\nQExHFX4_RSLC5_ADH_DSSO_stepped_ID.raw\nQExHFX4_RSLC5_Conalbumin_DSSO_stepped_ID.raw\nQExHFX4_RSLC5_Ovalbumin_DSSO_stepped_ID.raw\nQexHF1_RSLC1_Ribosome_ID.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nOptimized fragmentation improves the identification of peptides cross-linked using MS-cleavable reagents\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCross-linking mass spectrometry (XLMS) is becoming increasingly popular, and current advances are widening the applicability of the technique so that it can be utilized by non-specialist laboratories. Specifically, the use of novel mass spectrometry-cleavable (MS-cleavable) reagents dramatically reduces complexity of the data by providing i) characteristic reporter ions and ii) the mass of the individual peptides, rather than that of the cross-linked moiety. However, optimum acquisition strategies to obtain the best quality data for such cross-linkers with higher energy C-trap dissociation (HCD) alone is yet to be achieved. Therefore, we have carefully investigated and optimized MS parameters to facilitate the identification of disuccinimidyl sulfoxide (DSSO)-based cross-links on HCD-equipped mass spectrometers. From the comparison of 9 different fragmentation energies we chose several stepped- HCD fragmentation methods that were evaluated on a variety of cross-linked proteins. The optimal stepped-HCD method was then directly compared with previously described methods using an Orbitrap Fusion™ Lumos™ Tribrid™ instrument using a high-complexity sample. The final results indicate that our stepped-HCD method is able to identify more cross-links than other methods, mitigating the need for multistage MS (MSn) enabled instrumentation and alternative dissociation techniques.\n\n[METHODS]\nProteins were dissolved in XL-buffer (50 mM HEPES, 50 mM KCl, pH 7.5, the ribosome sample contained additionally 10 mM MgAc2) and cross-linked separately at a concentration of 1 µg/µl and a final DSSO concentration of 500 µM. The reaction was carried out for 1 h at room-temperature, before it was quenched with 50 mM Tris-HCL, pH 7.5. Samples were reduced with dithiothreitol (DTT, 10 mM, 30 min, 60 °C) and alkylated with iodoacetamide (IAA, 15 mM, 30 min rt in the dark). Alkylation was stopped by the addition of 5 mM DTT and proteins were digested with trypsin overnight (protein/enzyme 30:1, 37 °C). Digestion was stopped by the addition of trifluoracetic acid to a final concentration of 1% (v/v, pH <2) and samples were stored at -80 °C. The sample for stepped-HCD comparison was obtained by mixing all digests in an equimolar ratio. For the sequential digest, tryptic peptides were desalted using self-made C-18 Stage Tips, dissolved in XL-buffer and digested with S. aureus Protease V8 (GluC) for 4 hours (protein/enzyme 30:1, 37 °C). The ribosome sample was enriched for XLs prior to LC-MS/MS analysis using SEC. 25 µg of the digest were separated on a TSKgel SuperSW2000 column (300 mm × 4.5 mm × 4 μm, Tosoh Bioscience). (Figure S8) The three high mass fractions were subsequently measured via LC-MS/MS. Reversed-Phase HPLC. Digested peptides were separated using a Dionex UltiMate 3000 HPLC RSLC nanosystem prior to MS analysis. The HPLC was interfaced with the mass spectrometer via a Nanospray Flex™ ion source. For sample concentrating, washing and desalting, the peptides were trapped on an Acclaim PepMap C-18 precolumn (0.3x5mm, Thermo Fisher Scientific), using a flowrate of 25 µl/min and 100% buffer A (99.9% H2O, 0.1% TFA). The separation was performed on an Acclaim PepMap C-18 column (50 cm x 75 µm, 2 µm particles, 100 Ä pore size, Thermo Fisher Scientific) applying a flowrate of 230 nl/min. For separation, a solvent gradient ranging from 2-35% buffer B (80% ACN, 19.92% H2O, 0.08% TFA) was applied. The applied gradient varied from 60-180 min, depending on the sample complexity. Mass Spectrometry: All measurements involving DSSO and DSBU optimization and comparison were performed on an Orbitrap Fusion™ Lumos™ Tribrid™ (Thermo Fisher Scientific) mass spectrometer. Identification of the proteins contained in the ribosome sample, as well as the identification of cross-links from BSA (DSBU), ADH (DSSO), conalbumin (DSSO) and ovalbumin (DSSO) were per-formed on QExactive™ Orbitrap™ (Thermo Fisher Scientific) mass spectrometers. For detailed procedures for see Supplementary Information. For DSSO-XL identification, digested proteins were analyzed using the CID-ETD acquisition method described by Liu et al. Full scans were recorded at resolution 60000 and a scan range from 375-1500 m/z (AGC 4e5, max injection time 50 ms). MS/MS scans were recorded at 30000 resolution (AGC 5e4, max injection time 100 ms for CID and 120 ms ETD, isolation width 1.6 m/z). Singly and doubly charged ions were excluded from fragmentation since cross-linked pep-tides tend to occur at a charge state of 3+ or above.21 CID fragmentation energy was set to 25 % NCE and for ETD calibrated charge dependent ETD parameters were used. In CID-ETD acquisition, two subsequent fragmentation events using the complementary fragmentation strategies were triggered. All precursors that have been selected for fragmentation were excluded from fragmentation for 30 s. For the MSn acquisition strategy (from now on called MS2-MS3) the same settings as described above were used but only precursor with charge state 4-8+ were selected for MS/MS. The two most abundant reporter doublets from MS/MS scans (charge state 2-6, Δ-mass 31.9721 Da, ±30 ppm) were selected for MSn. MS3 scans were recorded in the ion trap operated in rapid mode with a maximum fill time of 150 ms (isolation width 2.0 m/z). Fragmentation was carried out using HCD with 30 % NCE. For stepped-HCD the settings described above were used with one adaptation. Ions for MS/MS were collected for a maximum of 150 ms. Selected precursors were fragmented applying a collision energy of 27±6 % NCE. NCE optimization: An inclusion list was generated that included all cross-links identified with CID-ETD using the Proteome Discoverer 2.2 output. Full scans were recorded at a resolution of 120000 ranging from 400-1600 m/z (AGC 2e5, 50 ms max. injection time). Only precursors from the inclusion list (10 min retention time window, matching charge and m/z [± 10 ppm]) were selected for fragmentation. MS/MS spectra were recorded at 30000 resolution (AGC 1e5, max. injection time 150 ms, isolation width 1.4 m/z). Each selected precursor was fragmented consecutively with 9 different NCEs and subsequently excluded from fragmentation for 30 s. The chronological order of fragmentation energies was randomly shuffled between the three injection replicates.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nStreptococcus pyogenes serotype m2; Cicer arietinum (chickpea) (garbanzo); Equus caballus (horse); Candida albicans (yeast); Escherichia coli; Homo sapiens (human); Oryctolagus cuniculus (rabbit); Bos taurus (bovine)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": 